The **Material Flow Controller**, or MFC, tracks inventory, creates tasks, assigns robots, and plans routes across the shared grid. Robot firmware owns motor control and immediate hardware safety.

The assistant is a chat window inside the MFC's web UI. It inspects relevant state, selects containers, constructs high-level requests, and explains a proposed plan. It does not plan routes, avoid collisions, recover tasks, or control firmware.

<figure>
  <Image
    src={assistantClarification}
    width={900}
    alt="The assistant reports a successful read-only boxes request and asks where to move the boxes from stack 5,1"
  />
  <figcaption><strong>Read first, ask when needed.</strong> The assistant inspects the stack through an allowed GET request, then asks the operator to resolve the missing destination.</figcaption>
</figure>

## 1. Warehouse credentials never reach the model

The assistant lives in the existing browser UI, so operators keep their existing permissions and the MFC needs no new backend or MCP server. They can use a supported provider such as local Ollama, OpenAI, Anthropic, or OpenRouter.

MFC authentication stays in the UI request layer and never enters the model prompt. The provider receives the operator's request and selected warehouse context, but no credential capable of calling the warehouse backend. Its own API key is stored separately in the browser and used only for model requests.

## 2. The model can request only allowlisted operations

The UI accepts a fixed set of high-level operations. Reads include executor status, robot and port states, tasks, containers, grid dimensions, and failed-action diagnostics. Writes are limited to selected task and configuration endpoints.

Low-level motor, gripper, and raw robot commands are excluded. Allowed writes still create MFC tasks, so the deterministic controller plans and validates the physical work. The UI checks the allowlist after every model response; mentioning another endpoint in JSON does not grant access.

## 3. Reads can run automatically; writes require review

The UI can run approved read-only requests automatically and return their results to the model for up to three iterations. Every `POST` becomes a visible plan containing a plain-language summary, endpoint, and JSON body.

<figure>
  <Image
    src={assistantPlan}
    width={900}
    alt="Plan preview listing 11 box movements with cancel, regenerate, and execute controls"
  />
  <figcaption><strong>The write boundary.</strong> All 11 proposed movements remain pending until the operator chooses to execute them.</figcaption>
</figure>

The operator must approve the exact plan before it reaches the MFC. Plans above 100 writes require another confirmation. This boundary catches ambiguous instructions that produce valid requests with the wrong intent.

## 4. A failed write stops the plan

Approved writes run sequentially. If one fails, the remaining writes are blocked rather than executed against an unexpected warehouse state. The result returns to the chat, where the model can explain the failure or propose a revised plan.

**Conclusion:** the LLM is valuable at the language boundary, where ambiguity is expected and every proposal can be reviewed. Deterministic software remains responsible for authorization, validation, execution, routing, and physical safety.

